    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm trying to put together a travel brief for a group of engineers heading through SE Asia (Singapore, Malaysia, Indonesia, Thailand, and Vietnam) next month. I'm getting really confused with the visa requirements. Can you help me clarify the difference between a transit visa and an entry visa for these specific countries? I need to make sure we don't have anyone getting held up at customs or denied boarding because they didn't have the right paperwork for a quick layover.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. An entry visa allows a traveler to reside temporarily in the host country, needing to be approved for the specific time they intend to stay. A transit visa, however, is only permitted if the traveler doesn't intend to leave through the paperless system and is traveling through the country while making a connection. It's essential to apply for country-specific visas well in advance as they can be quite strict. For Malaysia, it's mandatory to apply for a visa before arrival unless you're a citizen of some countries that are exempt ('Visa Exempt'). Travel to Singapore, Indonesia, Thailand, and Vietnam typically requires an entry visa unless you fall into the exempt category. Always check the most current requirements, as they can vary and change.",
      "source": "target",
